+# Owned protected backup root provider
+
+The Main composition can read an existing recovery destination from the exact
+owned Host and an immutable selector sealed in the existing Supervisor device
+store. Fresh registration and directory creation remain on HOLD because no
+original parent-namespace custody is available.
+The selector does not prove a backup or restore and grants no upgrade, rollback,
+database overwrite or installation permission. W74/W76, the original request/job
+registries, the native producer and the existing gates remain mandatory.
+
+## Selection and original authority
+
+`owned-protected-backup-root.mjs` accepts only the current controller-owned Host
+summary and the already loaded enrolled Supervisor identity. Its opaque provider
+and registration scopes are process-local registry entries; copied objects,
+matching JSON, callbacks and renderer paths cannot create them.
+
+The controller retains the exact configuration path and digest read at launch.
+Its fixed recovery reader requires that same child and generation, unchanged
+bounded configuration bytes, native protected-storage readback and the original
+status file's state directory. The owner-file/environment discovery helper is
+not recovery authority. A changed launch configuration requires a fresh owned
+Host; the running child never adopts newly selected bytes.
+
+The root is the fixed `protected-backup-root-v1` child of that private state
+directory. It cannot equal or overlap PGDATA. Parent, source and root use the
+existing `readDirectoryIdentity` encoding of volume/file/birth-time identity;
+content changes do not manufacture a different directory identity.
+
+## Read-only registration and preservation
+
+The existing `encrypted_protected_backup_root_record_b64` field is hidden from
+public snapshots and preserves the canonical client/device/key and native
+state/source/root identities. Existing READY records may be requalified by
+read-only observations. Existing REGISTERING records remain terminal HOLD.
+
+The selector has no registration-write or directory-creation branch. If the
+sealed record is absent, it throws
+`owned_protected_backup_root_original_parent_namespace_custody_required` before
+generating a registration ID, persisting REGISTERING or creating any directory.
+Every actual selector scope hardcodes `registration:null`; therefore the
+retained identity persistence method refuses before encryption or `#persist`.
+The transition/CAS machinery is dormant in production and its synthetic scope
+algorithm tests do not supply a current original namespace issuer.
+
+A final identity/ACL read followed by `mkdir(path)` cannot retain the parent
+namespace. A parent or ancestor may be replaced between those operations; even
+an immediately following post-check cannot undo an earlier write. No new key,
+trust root, callback or reported lease is introduced to bridge this gap. The
+required original interface must hold the Host-state and identity-store parent
+namespaces before the first write, perform fixed-name exclusive creation
+relative to that held parent, and retain the exact new child through the sealed
+commit and cleanup. See the required contract in
+[Recovery directory custody required](RECOVERY_DIRECTORY_CUSTODY_REQUIRED.md).
+
+Missing/replaced registered roots, malformed seals, identity changes and
+interrupted registration fail closed. An uncertain state replacement poisons
+the identity cache through its existing persistence fence. A later process cannot
+complete a leftover `REGISTERING`, recreate a missing registered root or rotate
+to another destination. READY requalification after restart requires the same
+sealed client/device/key and native state/source/root identities.
+
+Existing backup contents are retained. The selector never removes, rewrites or
+rotates them. The original producer's one-shot reservation and existing effect
+barriers still decide whether work can begin. Reusing the same protected
+directory is not permission to replay a backup or overwrite current data.
+
+## Main and retained request integration
+
+`bindOwnedRuntimeHostRecoveryComposition` uses this fixed read-only selector; caller path,
+ACL and proof callbacks are no longer composition inputs. Main passes a mounted
+composition into its owned capability. If original authority is unavailable,
+recovery remains unmounted while unrelated native admission keeps its existing
+guards. Missing registration is explicitly unmounted with
+`original_parent_namespace_custody_required`. There is no recovery fallback from
+a failed original selector.
+
+The original Main recovery provider requires the opaque selector. It rechecks
+the sealed physical root before binding W74 and before each root observation.
+Close revokes the selector and drain joins its outstanding reads. Original Main
+monotonic expiry, Host phase restriction, native-job identity and terminal UNKNOWN
+semantics remain in force. Root acquisition never refunds elapsed request time.
+
+No database connection, migration, dump, restore, native installation, updater
+actuation or trust-root replacement is performed by the selector. Database
+freshness and preservation still belong to the existing producer and gates;
+directory identity alone says nothing about database contents.
+
+## Validation and remaining qualification
+
+The source-only Linux validation contains 572 passing focused tests and 299
+source syntax checks. It includes real temporary-directory identities, zero-write
+selector/real-scope regressions, explicit mock-issuer sealed-state CAS algorithm
+tests, original controller/child lifecycle tests, real selector-to-Main integration, and existing
+W74/W76, handoff, phase, deadline and upgrade-gate regressions. Native Windows,
+Guardian and database authority boundaries in positive tests are explicitly
+synthetic; they do not attest an installed runtime.
+
+Package tests report 1 pass and 18 setup failures because the source snapshot
+lacks `infra/client-state-runtime/node_modules/postgres` (`postgres@3.4.7`). No
+dependency was installed. Independent static source-closure checks admit the new
+provider and all its dependencies; full packaging verification remains blocked.
+
+Installed Windows/ACL and PostgreSQL qualification were not run. Path observations
+do not provide an atomic native directory-handle lease across filesystem races.
+The selected producer's physical retention duties remain unchanged. The separate
+production Host recovery-service mount still depends on `hooks.createRecoveryService`;
+this patch does not supply that service or add a new user recovery command.
+These results do not establish installation or upgrade readiness.
+
+## Effect custody correction
+
+Consumer tracing confirmed that the selected producer/native adapter has no
+retained kernel handles for backup/PGDATA and their mutable ancestry. The
+current source therefore applies a fixed veto before source-pool allocation or
+any backup/restore effect, and before standalone native-adapter construction.
